+# Pre-ship review — USDC Payouts dApp (frontend + config)
+
+Reviewed: `packages/nextjs` (app, config, providers) and the deploy/CI config that
+affects it. Contract `Payouts.sol` read only for context; it is correct and in scope
+only where the frontend contradicts it.
+
+**What I ran:** `yarn install`, `yarn next:check-types` (clean), `yarn next:lint`
+(0 errors, **100 prettier warnings**), `yarn next:build` (succeeds, 8 routes). No
+live run against Base — no RPC key in the repo — so the RPC findings below are
+derived from the code paths, not observed.
+
+**Verdict: do not ship as-is.** The build is green, but `/payouts` moves real USDC
+with no chain guard, no error surfacing, and an event query that will bury the RPC
+endpoint on first page load.
+
+Counts: 7 ship-blocking, 14 should-fix.
+
+---
+
+## Ship-blocking
+
+### 1. Payout writes use raw wagmi — no wrong-chain guard, no simulation, no error path
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:6, 21, 38-43, 51-56`**
+
+The page calls wagmi's `useWriteContract` directly. wagmi submits to whatever chain
+the **wallet** is on, and `wagmiConfig` deliberately enables Ethereum mainnet
+alongside Base for ENS (`services/web3/wagmiConfig.tsx:11-14`). So a user whose
+wallet is on mainnet gets a fully live Approve / Send Payout form. Neither
+`0x7c3f2A9d…` (Payouts) nor `0x833589fC…` (Base USDC) has code on mainnet, so the
+calls succeed as no-ops: gas burned, form cleared, no payout, no error. The balance
+above the form is meanwhile pinned to Base, so the UI actively reassures them.
+
+The header does render `WrongNetworkDropdown` in this state
+(`components/scaffold-eth/RainbowKitCustomConnectButton/index.tsx:41-43`), but the
+page ignores it and the buttons stay enabled.
+
+**Fix:** replace both calls with the SE-2 hooks:
+```ts
+const { writeContractAsync: writeUsdc, isMining: isApproving } = useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "USDC" });
+const { writeContractAsync: writePayouts, isMining: isPaying } = useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "Payouts" });
+```
+`useScaffoldWriteContract` refuses to send when `accountChain.id !== targetNetwork.id`
+(`hooks/scaffold-eth/useScaffoldWriteContract.ts:98-105`), simulates first
+(`:118-124`), and routes failures through `notification.error`. This single change
+also resolves findings 3, 4 and part of 8.
+
+### 2. `fromBlock: 0n` scans all of Base mainnet on every page load
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:32`**
+
+`useScaffoldEventHistory` pages history in `blocksBatchSize`-sized chunks, default
+**500 blocks** (`hooks/scaffold-eth/useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:95, 156, 183`), each
+one a sequential `eth_getLogs`. Base head is tens of millions of blocks, so
+`fromBlock: 0n` means on the order of 10^5 round-trips per visitor before the feed
+renders anything. In practice: the RPC key is rate-limited within seconds, the feed
+never loads, and every other read on the page (balance, block number) starves behind
+it.
+
+`deployedContracts.ts:48` already carries `deployedOnBlock: 33712450`, and the hook
+falls back to it when `fromBlock` is omitted (`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:128-135`).
+
+**Fix (minimum):** delete the `fromBlock: 0n` line and raise the batch size:
+```ts
+const { data: payouts } = useScaffoldEventHistory({
+  contractName: "Payouts",
+  eventName: "Paid",
+  watch: true,
+  blocksBatchSize: 10_000,
+});
+```
+**Fix (correct):** the hook is `@deprecated` for exactly this reason —
+"*Recommended only for local (hardhat/anvil) chains… it uses getLogs which can
+overload RPC endpoints (especially on L2s)*"
+(`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:57-60`). Before real users, back the feed with an
+indexer (ponder or a subgraph — both have skills under `.agents/skills/`). Even
+from `deployedOnBlock` this is ~1M+ blocks of catch-up per visitor.
+
+### 3. Every error is swallowed into `console.error`
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:44-46, 59-61`**
+
+User rejects in the wallet → nothing. Allowance too low → nothing. Balance too low →
+nothing. Malformed recipient → nothing. Wrong chain → nothing. The user sees a button
+stop spinning and no explanation. This is the single biggest source of "I clicked
+send and nothing happened" support tickets you will get on Friday.
+
+**Fix:** the app already ships the plumbing —
+```ts
+import { getParsedError, notification } from "~~/utils/scaffold-eth";
+// ...
+} catch (e) {
+  notification.error(getParsedError(e));
+}
+```
+Or get it for free via `useScaffoldWriteContract` (finding 1).
+
+### 4. No pending/success feedback, and the form clears before the tx confirms
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:57-58`**
+
+`setAmount("")` / `setMemo("")` run the moment the wallet returns a hash — not when
+the transaction is mined. If it reverts, the inputs are already gone and nothing on
+screen says so. There is no toast, no explorer link, no "mining" state; with a 30s
+poll (finding 16) the feed can take half a minute to show the payout, so the user's
+only signal that anything worked is that the form emptied itself.
+
+**Fix:** `useScaffoldWriteContract` wraps `useTransactor`, which shows
+"waiting for confirmation" → "transaction completed" with a block-explorer link.
+Clear the form in the confirmation callback, not inline:
+```ts
+await writePayouts(
+  { functionName: "pay", args: [recipient, parsedAmount, memo] },
+  { blockConfirmations: 1, onBlockConfirmation: () => { setAmount(""); setMemo(""); } },
+);
+```
+
+### 5. Recipient address is never validated
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:17, 55, 78-84`**
+
+A free-text input is cast straight through: `recipient as \`0x${string}\``. A
+malformed value makes viem throw inside the `try`, which finding 3 then silently
+eats. A well-formed but wrong address is accepted with no second look and no
+confirmation step — and a USDC transfer is irreversible.
+
+**Fix:** use `AddressInput` from `@scaffold-ui/components` (ENS resolution + built-in
+validation, and it is what AGENTS.md mandates), guard with viem's `isAddress`, and
+disable "Send Payout" until the address is valid. For a money-moving action, add a
+confirm step that echoes the resolved address and amount.
+
+### 6. Approve / Send are allowance-blind
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:36-62, 99-114`**
+
+Both buttons are always enabled and nothing indicates which one is needed. `approve`
+is granted for exactly the current `amount` (`:42`), so the natural flow —
+approve 100, change the field to 200, hit Send — reverts on
+`Payouts: transfer failed`, silently (finding 3). Users will conclude the app is
+broken. Conversely a user who already has an allowance is told to approve again and
+pays gas for nothing.
+
+**Fix:** read the allowance and drive the UI off it:
+```ts
+const { data: allowance } = useScaffoldReadContract({
+  contractName: "USDC",
+  functionName: "allowance",
+  args: [connectedAddress, PAYOUTS.address],
+});
+const needsApproval = allowance === undefined || allowance < parsedAmount;
+```
+Render Approve only when `needsApproval`, disable Send until it is false. Approving
+`parsedAmount` exactly (rather than max-uint) is the right call — keep it, just make
+the state visible.
+
+### 7. Production runs on Scaffold-ETH's shared demo credentials
+**`packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:16, 29, 32-35, 40`**
+
+Neither `NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` nor `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` is
+set anywhere in the repo (only `.env.example`, no `.env.local`), so both fall back to
+the public SE-2 defaults that every scaffold project on the internet shares.
+
+Two consequences:
+- `rpcOverrides` is empty, and because `alchemyApiKey === DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY`,
+  `wagmiConfig.tsx:20` **de-prioritises** Alchemy to the back of the fallback list.
+  Base traffic therefore goes primarily to the chain default,
+  `https://mainnet.base.org` — a public endpoint that aggressively rate-limits.
+  Combined with finding 2 this is a guaranteed outage on launch day.
+- The default WalletConnect project id is not domain-verified for your deployment,
+  which degrades mobile wallet deep-linking and can surface an "unverified app"
+  warning at exactly the moment a user is about to approve USDC spending.
+
+**Fix:** provision your own Alchemy key and WalletConnect project id; set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` in the
+Vercel project env; verify the production domain in WalletConnect Cloud. Optionally
+pin the Base RPC explicitly:
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: { [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL ?? "" },
+```
+
+---
+
+## Should-fix
+
+### 8. Lint fails CI
+**`.github/workflows/lint.yaml:40`** runs `yarn next:lint --max-warnings=0`; the tree
+currently has **100 prettier warnings** (`scaffold.config.ts`, `wagmiConfig.tsx`,
+`getMetadata.ts`, `next.config.ts`, `types/abitype/abi.d.ts`). The job will go red on
+the next push to `main`.
+**Fix:** `yarn format`. Nothing else — `check-types` and `next build` both pass.
+
+### 9. One `isPending` drives both buttons
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:21, 102-112`**
+
+A single `useWriteContract` instance backs Approve and Send, so clicking either puts
+*both* into the disabled/loading state. The user cannot tell which action is in
+flight.
+**Fix:** two hook instances (see finding 1) with `isApproving` / `isPaying`.
+
+### 10. `btn loading` does not render a spinner in daisyUI 5
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:101, 108`**
+
+`package.json` pins `daisyui@5.5.19`. In v5 `loading` is a standalone component, not
+a button modifier — the v4 `btn loading` idiom is dead. The buttons currently just go
+disabled with no visual feedback. (The codebase already does this correctly in
+`utils/scaffold-eth/notification.tsx:28`.)
+**Fix:**
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isApproving}>
+  {isApproving && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-xs" />}
+  Approve
+</button>
+```
+
+### 11. Hardcoded dark palette breaks the app theme
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:65, 121`**
+
+`bg-black text-white` on a full-height wrapper, plus `border-gray-700` on the feed
+rows. The rest of the app is themed through daisyUI light/dark
+(`styles/globals.css:10-14`) with a working `SwitchTheme` toggle in the footer — in
+light mode `/payouts` is a black slab that matches nothing, and the toggle appears
+broken on that page. Also contradicts the "use daisyUI classes" rule in AGENTS.md.
+**Fix:** `bg-base-100 text-base-content`, `border-base-300`, and wrap the form in
+`card bg-base-100 shadow-xl` like the rest of the app.
+
+### 12. Page bypasses the SE-2 contract abstraction
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:7, 10-11`**
+
+```ts
+const PAYOUTS = deployedContracts[8453].Payouts;
+const USDC = deployedContracts[8453].USDC;
+```
+Chain 8453 is hardcoded at module scope. This breaks the moment a staging chain,
+Base Sepolia, or a local fork is added to `targetNetworks`, and duplicates what
+`useDeployedContractInfo` / the scaffold hooks already resolve from
+`scaffold.config.ts`. It is also what makes finding 1 possible — the addresses are
+decoupled from the connected chain.
+**Fix:** drop the direct import; address and ABI come from the `contractName` in the
+scaffold hooks. Where you genuinely need the Payouts address (the `allowance`
+spender), take it from `useDeployedContractInfo({ contractName: "Payouts" })`.
+
+### 13. Hand-rolled `shorten()` instead of the `Address` component
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:13, 122, 124`**
+
+Loses ENS names, blockie avatar, copy-to-clipboard and the block-explorer link — all
+of which the app already has via `@scaffold-ui/components` (used correctly on
+`app/page.tsx:27`). For a payouts feed, "did this go to the right person" is the
+whole question, and a truncated hex string is the worst possible answer.
+**Fix:** `<Address address={payout.args.to} size="sm" />`; delete `shorten`.
+
+### 14. Event list keyed by array index
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:120-121`**
+
+`key={i}` — but the hook **prepends** new events to the array
+(`useScaffoldEventHistory.ts:271`), so with `watch: true` every existing row's key
+shifts by one on each new payout. React then reuses DOM nodes across different
+payouts; with the `Address` component of finding 13 in place this shows up as rows
+briefly displaying the wrong avatar/ENS.
+**Fix:** `key={`${payout.transactionHash}-${payout.logIndex}`}`.
+
+### 15. Loading balance is displayed as `0`
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:75`**
+
+`usdcBalance !== undefined ? … : "0"` conflates "not fetched yet" with "you have no
+money". On a 30s poll (finding 16) with a cold cache, the first thing a user sees is
+`Your balance: 0 USDC`. Raw `formatUnits` also prints full 6-decimal precision
+(`1234.567891`).
+**Fix:** render a `skeleton` or `—` while `undefined`, and format for display:
+```tsx
+{usdcBalance === undefined ? <span className="skeleton h-4 w-16 inline-block" />
+  : Number(formatUnits(usdcBalance, 6)).toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: 2 })}
+```
+
+### 16. `pollingInterval: 30000` is wrong for an L2
+**`packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:24`**
+
+Base produces blocks every ~2s. At 30s the balance and the payout feed lag by up to
+half a minute, which — with no tx toast (finding 4) — is the entire feedback loop for
+"did my payout go through". AGENTS.md explicitly says to lower this for L2s.
+**Fix:** `pollingInterval: 4000`. Note the ordering: do **not** lower this until
+findings 2 and 7 are done, or you will simply hit the rate limit faster.
+
+### 17. Deploy path silently ignores TypeScript errors
+**`packages/nextjs/next.config.ts:6-8`** + **`packages/nextjs/package.json`
+(`vercel:yolo`)**
+
+`typescript.ignoreBuildErrors` is wired to `NEXT_PUBLIC_IGNORE_BUILD_ERROR`, which
+`vercel:yolo` sets to `true` — and AGENTS.md tells you to ship with
+`yarn vercel:yolo --prod`. That is a fine escape hatch for a hackathon and a bad one
+for a payouts app.
+**Fix:** deploy production with `yarn vercel --prod` (the non-yolo script). Types are
+currently clean, so this costs you nothing today and catches a real regression later.
+
+### 18. Default Scaffold-ETH branding is still everywhere
+- `app/layout.tsx:11-12` — `title: "Scaffold-ETH 2 App"`, description
+  `"Built with 🏗 Scaffold-ETH 2"`. This is the browser tab title and the OG card for
+  every link your users share.
+- `app/page.tsx:22, 30-45` — the landing page is the untouched scaffold splash:
+  *"Get started by editing packages/nextjs/app/page.tsx"*, *"Edit your smart contract
+  YourContract.sol"*. `/` is where users land.
+- `components/Header.tsx:92-97` — SE2 logo, "Scaffold-ETH / Ethereum dev stack".
+- `components/Footer.tsx:50-73` — "Fork me" → github.com/scaffold-eth/se-2, and a
+  **Support** link pointing at the Scaffold-ETH Telegram. Your users will take
+  payout problems there.
+
+**Fix:** real title/description/OG image in `getMetadata`, replace `/` with a real
+landing page (or redirect it to `/payouts`), swap the logo/wordmark, repoint or
+remove the footer links — the Support link especially.
+
+### 19. `/debug` and `/blockexplorer` ship to end users
+**`components/Header.tsx:29-33`**, **`app/page.tsx:55, 65`**
+
+"Debug Contracts" is in the main nav on every page. On Base mainnet it exposes raw
+`pay`, `approve`, `transfer` and `transferFrom` on live USDC to non-technical users
+with no guardrails — an easy way for someone to `approve` an arbitrary spender.
+`/blockexplorer` is worse-than-useless: it only works against local hardhat and
+greets a Base user with an error toast (`app/blockexplorer/page.tsx:17-40`), yet the
+home page links to it as a feature.
+**Fix:** gate both nav entries and the home-page cards on
+`targetNetwork.id === hardhat.id`, matching how `FaucetButton`
+(`Header.tsx:105`) and the footer's Block Explorer link (`Footer.tsx:33-41`) already
+do it. Ideally remove the routes from the production build entirely.
+
+### 20. Payout feed is global, unbounded, and renders attacker-controlled text
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:29-34, 118-131`**
+
+The feed renders *every* `Paid` event ever emitted, not the connected user's. Two
+issues:
+- `Payouts.pay` is permissionless, so anyone can emit a `Paid` event with an
+  arbitrary `memo` for a fraction of a cent. That free text lands verbatim in your
+  product's feed — an ideal phishing surface ("Payout failed, re-claim at …").
+  React escapes it so there is no XSS, but the social-engineering vector is real, and
+  an unbounded memo also blows out the layout.
+- There is no `.slice()`, so once volume picks up the page renders thousands of rows.
+
+**Fix:** filter to the user and cap the render:
+```ts
+useScaffoldEventHistory({ contractName: "Payouts", eventName: "Paid", watch: true,
+  filters: { from: connectedAddress } })
+```
+then `.slice(0, 25)` on render, and clamp the memo (`line-clamp-2` plus a length cap).
+If a team-wide feed is genuinely wanted, restrict it to a known sender allowlist
+rather than showing the raw contract history.
+
+### 21. Amount input accepts values the contract rejects
+**`packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:85-91`**
+
+A bare `type="number"` with no `min`/`step`. It accepts negatives (`parseUnits("-5", 6)`
+→ a negative that reverts on the uint256 boundary), exponent notation (`1e5`), and
+more than 6 decimals (viem rounds silently — the user sends a different amount than
+they typed). Nothing checks the value against `usdcBalance`, and empty input becomes
+`parseUnits("0")`, which reverts on `Payouts: zero amount` — silently, per finding 3.
+**Fix:** `min="0" step="0.01"` plus an explicit guard, or use `IntegerInput` /
+`EtherInput` from `@scaffold-ui/components`. Disable Send when
+`parsedAmount <= 0n || parsedAmount > (usdcBalance ?? 0n)` and show inline helper
+text for each case.
+
